 #uuid "bebe4460-e57e-546f-99c8-81192aafc1ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087774 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087774 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087774 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087774 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087774 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186265844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087774 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087774 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087774 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087774 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087774 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186444007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087774 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186444008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087774 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087774 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087774 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b4ad20bb-c6a4-5390-a11a-e5277cfd1667" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087944 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087944 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087944 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087944 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087944 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087944 #edb/ref 1045 "Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs: Characteristic Equations With Repeated Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087944 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186088643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087944 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087944 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087944 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "c7c1a0ce-38f8-537d-a8f0-5c255830b86e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088239 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088239 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64798" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088239 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088239 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the equation ${y}^{″}-14{y}^{′}+49y=0$ for $y=y(t),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088239 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "41fb4ba9-2a2a-520b-a6ad-5cdc344e0ff5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088239 #edb/ref 1074 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-14\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+49{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-14\\lambda +49) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-14\\lambda +49 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -7)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =7$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{7t}$ and $t{e}^{7t}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $7{e}^{t}+t{e}^{7t}$ is not a solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088643 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088643 #edb/ref 1061 24226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088643 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0a076b74-a850-5e47-b647-e51c36c1259a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088643 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088644 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088644 #edb/ref 1061 24228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088644 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f1046776-23ad-5801-b3b5-f777cbc55e3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088644 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088645 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088645 #edb/ref 1061 24229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088645 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e78e8b59-e0a4-5f68-8156-3b7d5844fde6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088645 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088646 #edb/ref 1061 24230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088646 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8392881d-182b-5602-83c1-1fbe45b22882" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088646 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088941 #edb/ref 1064 "When the characteristic equation of a second-order homogeneous ODE has a **repeated root** $\\lambda = c,$ the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x) = Ae^{cx} + Bxe^{cx}.\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's solve the following ODE:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 9y =0.\n$$\n\nAssuming a solution of the form $y=e^{\\lambda x},$ we differentiate and substitute into the ODE:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-6\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+9{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{\\lambda x}$ is never zero, we can divide by it to get the **characteristic equation**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $\\lambda = 3$ is a **repeated root**. Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x) = Ae^{3x} + Bxe^{3x}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088941 #edb/ref 1065 4049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088941 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a4fc9c29-f54a-5346-b7b3-59abdf8bb39f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088941 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119758 #edb/ref 103